			<div class="content" id="post-content-2346441">
									

<blockquote>
<p>I have no idea how he knows so much about Nazis. Is history education just that much superior in the Netherlands (Crayne lives in the Netherlands, right)? Is it just a Europe thing? Idunno. His feedback was fantastic, as was his enthusiasm.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Well, there's a couple of reasons (and trust me, I am not an expert by any means).</p>
<ol>
<li>Dutch high school is available at a couple of levels, the highest being VWO (Preparatory Scientific Education), and at that level, history education is both broad and in-depth.</li>
<li>The Netherlands are literally right next to Germany, and were invaded during the Second World War. This makes it a pretty prominent subject in history education.</li>
<li>I have always been interested in WWII, my grandfathers and -mothers having lived through it and having told me stories about both the hardships and the good things that came from it. In addition, they never thought in terms of good and evil, black and white, they thought in terms of people and unfortunate circumstances. I think it was that approach to their experiences that really fostered my interest. If they hadn't, WWII would just have been real life Star Wars to a little kid.</li>
<li>I have two WWII/Occult Nazi-tech scips on the site, so I've needed to do a lot of research in order to make stuff feel somewhat believable (which is a pretty odd thing to say really, considering the subject ;)), so I know a few things. And I mean a few. Again, I am not an expert by any means.</li>
</ol>

